[38;2;124;124;124mdistribution, is it true that $f$ is necessarily bounded by a polynomial [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfunction?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA tempered distribution is a continuous linear functional on the Schwartz space [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^n)$. A locally integrable function $f$ defines a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtempered distribution if the functional $T_f(\varphi) = \int f(x)\varphi(x)dx$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mis well-defined and continuous on $\mathcal{S}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question is: if $f$ is continuous and defines a tempered distribution, must [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$f$ be polynomially bounded (i.e., $|f(x)| \leq C(1+|x|)^N$ for some $C, N$)?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe answer is NO. There exist continuous functions that are tempered [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdistributions but are not polynomially bounded.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think of a counterexample.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mConsider a function that has tall, thin spikes. The spikes can be very tall [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(growing faster than any polynomial) but very thin, so that the integral against[0m[38;2;124;124;124many Schwartz function is still finite and the functional is continuous.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me construct such a function. Consider $f(x) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\phi_n(x)$ where $\phi_n$ are bump functions centered at $n$ with very narrow [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msupport and $a_n$ grows very fast.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet's say $\phi_n$ is supported in $[n - \delta_n, n + \delta_n]$ with $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\phi_n(n) = 1$, and $a_n = e^{n^2}$ or something super-polynomial.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $f$ to be a tempered distribution, we need $T_f(\varphi) = \sum a_n \int [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\phi_n(x) \varphi(x) dx$ to converge for all $\varphi \in \mathcal{S}$ and to be[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcontinuous.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$\int \phi_n(x) \varphi(x) dx \leq \|\phi_n\|_1 \sup_{x \in [n-\delta_n, n+[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\delta_n]} |\varphi(x)|$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf $\phi_n$ is a bump of height 1 and width $2\delta_n$, then $\|\phi_n\|_1 [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\approx 2\delta_n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $\varphi \in \mathcal{S}$, $|\varphi(x)| \leq C_k (1+|x|)^{-k}$ for any $k$.[0m[38;2;124;124;124mSo $|\varphi(n)| \leq C_k (1+n)^{-k}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $|a_n \int \phi_n \varphi| \leq a_n \cdot 2\delta_n \cdot C_k (1+n)^{-k}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe need $\sum a_n \delta_n (1+n)^{-k} < \infty$ for all $k$ (and the bound to be[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcontrolled by Schwartz seminorms).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf we choose $\delta_n$ small enough, e.g., $\delta_n = e^{-n^2} \cdot (1+n)^{-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mn}$ or something, then even with $a_n = e^{n^2}$, we get $a_n \delta_n = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(1+n)^{-n}$ which decays faster than any polynomial.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me be more careful. We need the sum to converge for every $\varphi[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\in \mathcal{S}$, and we need continuity: $|T_f(\varphi)| \leq C \sum_{|[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\alpha|,|\beta| \leq N} \rho_{\alpha,\beta}(\varphi)$ for some $N, C$, where $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\rho_{\alpha,\beta}(\varphi) = \sup_x |x^\alpha \partial^\beta \varphi(x)|$ are [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe Schwartz seminorms.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me take $f(x) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} e^{n^2} \psi_n(x)$ where $\psi_n$ is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnon-negative smooth bump supported in $[n - \delta_n, n+\delta_n]$ with $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\psi_n(n) = 1$ and $\|\psi_n\|_\infty = 1$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mChoose $\delta_n = e^{-2n^2}$. Then:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$|T_f(\varphi)| \leq \sum_n e^{n^2} \int \psi_n(x) |\varphi(x)| dx \leq \sum_n [0m
[38;2;124;124;124me^{n^2} \cdot 2\delta_n \cdot \sup_{|x-n|\leq \delta_n} |\varphi(x)|$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$= \sum_n e^{n^2} \cdot 2e^{-2n^2} \cdot \sup_{|x-n|\leq \delta_n} |\varphi(x)| [0m[38;2;124;124;124m= \sum_n 2e^{-n^2} \sup_{|x-n|\leq \delta_n} |\varphi(x)|$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow $\sup_{|x-n|\leq \delta_n} |\varphi(x)| \leq \sup_{x} |\varphi(x)| = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\rho_{0,0}(\varphi)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $|T_f(\varphi)| \leq 2\rho_{0,0}(\varphi) \sum_n e^{-n^2} < \infty$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis shows $T_f$ is continuous on $\mathcal{S}$ (bounded by a single Schwartz [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mseminorm). So $f$ is a tempered distribution.[0m

